>L wrote:
>> Here's _my_ way to make NetHack harder:
>>
>> * Two words: stronger monsters. Currently the only creatures capable of
>> busting a character at midgame time are jabberwocks and minotaurs. Tut,
>> tut. If the "Hell-only" monsters' attacks contained several d10's or
>> d20's, we'd solve half of the problem right there.
>
>The problem here is that simple damage just isn't a deterrent to people
>who want to power up forever.  

  A better question is why people want to power up forever.
And so what if people power up forever?  It won't save them
from instadeaths, so it's just one more resistance to another 
form of death.

>Altar camp until you have a bazillion HP.  

  I think there's pretty much one reason to get a bazillion hit 
points.  It's so the player can focus on the real threat: 
instadeaths.

  NB: You can die with a bazillion hit points.

>And it penalizes speed runs and other interesting approaches
>disproportionately.  

  Not nearly as much as your proposals would.

>I'd be more in favor of limiting how much the
>player can buff up--a max HP cap, or diminishing returns so that the
>probability of getting more max HP from altars/potions/etc goes down
>the higher your HP are.

  There already is a point of diminishing returns: much over one
hundred hit points, two hundred if you don't feel lucky.

  One thousand hit points is like poison resistance, it protects
you against one particular form of dying.  Get mobbed five deep
in the middle of the Plane of Air without some means of 
teleporting monsters away and it just guarantees the slowest,
most boring death you can imagine.  

  I actually watched a player in this situation on NAO.  
Eventually died to genocidal confusion.  On purpose?

  It's not an instawin.  It won't save against instadeaths.  If 
players want to waste their time and resources getting one 
thousand hit points, so what?  

  It's also a limited resistance, where the player chooses just
how resistant he want's to be against death by hit point loss.

>
>That said, a few more rare hard-hitting monsters that a skilled player
>could avoid or deal with from afar make sense as a deterrent to someone
>blindly doing Valk Smash!  Make some monsters that are immune to
>physical weapons, so there's a reason to actually use up attack wands
>in combat (and to save some in the mid-game).

  Punishing less-skilled players.  And making "some monsters that
are immune to physical weapons" is just turning Gehennom into
a puzzle game.  Memorize the solution to the puzzle and it
becomes boring.

  Right now, Juiblex is like that.  I've seen monsters like this
referred to as "October monsters."  They're almost invulnerable
until you say "October" to them.  Then they fall over dead.

>
>Eliminate Elbereth in Gehennom--the lady can't help you here.

  Elbereth in Gehennom is like having four-wheel drive in a 
swamp:  It just means you'll be in *real* trouble when it's
no longer enough.  

  I watched one guy on NAO carefully using Elbereth outside he 
Castle until he was surrounded by soldiers.  He'd also carefully 
let himself get trapped against the moat so that a couatl
instakilled him.



